{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\section{Method}

\subsection{Task Definition}

We study \emph{verdict prediction}: given a Python function, a test input, and an optional execution trace, predict whether the test passes or fails.
The model produces a single token---\texttt{pass} or \texttt{fail}---making this a binary classification task.
Unlike code generation \citep{chen2025tracepile} or output prediction \citep{gu2024cruxeval}, verdict prediction isolates fault detection from execution simulation.

\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

We sampled 10 candidate solutions per problem from Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct (temperature 0.8, top\_p 0.95) for 591 problems from HumanEval \citep{chen2021evaluating} (164) and MBPP-sanitized \citep{austin2021program} (427), executed each with \texttt{sys.settrace} to obtain line-level traces and pass/fail verdicts, yielding 5,910 pairs (67\% pass, 33\% fail).
The dataset is split 80/20 by problem ID into 4,720 training and 1,190 test samples; majority-class baseline is 71.51\%.
The \texttt{exception\_only} format collapses multiple passing solutions into identical rows (39.45\% train duplication, 48.99\% test), since correct executions produce no exception events regardless of solution logic.
Train--test input overlap is zero (split by problem ID).

\subsection{Trace Representations}

We define seven input conditions that systematically vary the trace information available (Figure~\ref{fig:trace_overview}):
\texttt{no\_trace} (source code only),
\texttt{full\_trace} (complete line-by-line trace),
\texttt{VOTA} (exception events + assertion-point comparisons + loop compression),
\texttt{exception\_only} (only exception/error events),
\texttt{ae\_only} (only assertion-point actual/expected pairs),
\texttt{loop\_only} (only loop compression summaries),
and \texttt{VOTA\_no\_ae} (VOTA without assertion-point information).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_trace_overview}
\caption{Overview of the verdict prediction pipeline. Four conditions vary the trace information: \texttt{no\_trace} (75.13\%), \texttt{full\_trace} (81.76\%), VOTA (99.41\%), and \texttt{exception\_only} (99.83\%). Accuracies: Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, seed=42, $N{=}1{,}190$.}
\label{fig:trace_overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate two instruction-tuned code LLMs at the 7B scale:
\textbf{Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct} \citep{qwen2025coder} (5.5T pretraining tokens, primary model for all ablations) and
\textbf{DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Instruct} \citep{deepseek2024coder} (2T tokens, cross-model validation).

\subsection{Training Setup}

We fine-tune with LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} applied to all linear layers (rank=16, $\alpha$=32); full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_details}.
All conditions use identical configurations: 3 epochs, effective batch size 32, learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$ with linear decay, max sequence length 2{,}048 tokens.
Input sequences exceeding this limit are truncated from the input content while preserving the verdict label.
The primary seed is 42; robustness seeds are 123 and 456.

\subsection{Loss Ablation}

To disentangle training signal quality from input signal quality, we introduce a \texttt{full\_trace\_label\_only} variant that uses the same full trace as input but computes loss only on the verdict token.
If this improves over standard \texttt{full\_trace}, the improvement is attributable to \emph{gradient dilution}; the remaining gap to VOTA is then attributable to \emph{information overload}.
